{
 "id": "2026-11-21-cop31-overtime-fossil-fuels",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "COP thirty-one was supposed to end Friday. Why are nearly two hundred countries still negotiating?"
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "The climate talks in Antalya, Türkiye, ran past their Friday deadline, The National reports. The sticking point: Thursday's draft deal. Several outlets report it no longer mentions fossil fuels, and dropped a one hundred twenty billion dollar target to help poorer countries adapt."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "Countries including France, Panama and the Solomon Islands wrote that any deal must include a clear transition away from fossil fuels. Panama's environment minister, Juan Carlos Navarro, threatened a formal objection. Reports say resistance is led by producers, including Saudi Arabia and Russia."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "COP president Murat Kurum said: we are close to the finish line, but we are not there yet. Talks chief Chris Bowen said he would work as long as it takes. A new draft was expected Saturday."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will the final COP thirty-one deal mention fossil fuels? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
